  "enhanced_information": "The patient, a 29-year-old pregnant woman at 23 weeks gestation, presents with symptoms suggestive of nephrolithiasis (kidney stones), including left flank pain migrating to the groin, sharp pain, nausea, vomiting, and urinary frequency. The urinalysis shows significant hematuria (3+ blood, 291 RBCs/ul) and pyuria (75 WBCs/ul), which supports the suspicion of a urinary tract obstruction or kidney stones. In pregnant patients, it is crucial to avoid radiation exposure when possible. Therefore, the standard consensus is to use renal ultrasound as the first-line imaging modality to evaluate for nephrolithiasis or urinary tract obstruction, as it is safe during pregnancy and can effectively identify stones or obstructions. The retrieved evidence paths confirm the association of urinalysis findings with kidney calculi and urinary tract obstruction, supporting the use of renal ultrasound. Thus, the next best step in management is a renal ultrasound.",
